{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794186443,"nonce":"hNKWvLIoCpvVbwYJGpBM8vz/LAM=","title":"Pipeline constants: one artifact per set of values, decided at build time","message":"naga's writers refuse modules that still declare pipeline-overridable constants; wgpu resolves them when it creates a pipeline, which is run-time compilation. russl resolves every #[pipeline] constant to its default before writing the targets (naga's process_overrides, then the emptied override arena is validated again), so today each module yields one specialisation.\n\nBlinc's SDF pipelines depend on more than one: sdf_core.wgsl's FEAT_* switches build a full and a simple pipeline from one source. Offline, each set of values must be its own artifact.\n\nOptions to decide:\n1. An attribute listing the variants, e.g. #![variants(simple(FEAT_SHADOW = false, FEAT_MASK = false))], each emitted as its own set of consts (WGSL_SIMPLE, MSL_SIMPLE, ...) or as a struct per variant.\n2. Plain const generics on the module once modules and generics land: russl!(sdf_core\u003cFEAT_SHADOW = false\u003e).\n3. Keep overrides in the WGSL output only (WebGPU and wgpu accept them) and specialise only the native targets; WebGPU then compiles at pipeline creation, which browsers do anyway.\n\nGate: Blinc's CoreSimple pipeline built from russl artifacts with no override processing at run time.","files":null}]}